{"Service.Check returns immediately on a permDenialCache hit, so a previously cached denial will override the new 'requery DB on stale permissions' behavior and can keep denying for the cache TTL even after the DB would allow, reintroducing post-change access issues for up to ~30s": 0.8923421502113342, "userPermDenialCacheKey concatenates action/name/parent with '_' without escaping or length-prefixing, so different (name,parent) tuples can generate identical cache keys, applying a cached denial to the wrong resource and incorrectly denying access": 0.8805504441261292, "permDenialCache entries are keyed by (user, action, name, parent) where name and parent come directly from the request, so distinct denied resource names/folders produce distinct cache entries causing high-cardinality cache churn and memory/eviction pressure": 0.7476277947425842, "The denial cache key uses checkReq.UserUID parsed from the subject string, but permission caching uses the canonical userIdentifiers.UID resolved via GetUserIdentifiers, causing mismatch that can produce multiple denial keys for the same user identity and reduces cache consistency": 0.4628111720085144}